# The Effects of Polysemy for Japanese Katakana Words.

**Authors:** Hino, Yasushi, Lupker, Stephen J., Sears, Chris R., Ogawa, Taeko

## Abstract
Observes in a lexical decision task that polysemy effects were identical for high- and low-frequency katakana words; and that in a naming task, although no word frequency effect was observed, there was a significant polysemy effect which was identical for high- and low-frequency words. Discusses implications about the loci of such polysemy and frequency effects. (SR)

**Source:** https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ579311

